Start Rooms on your machine, join a room as two people, open a direct
conversation the room is not part of, and then open your whole session on
another device.

# Start Rooms

From the glowcap checkout, run:

```sh
zig build rooms-web
```

This builds the services, the browser's WebAssembly, and the interface, then
serves the page at `http://localhost:8080`. Peers reach the server over
encrypted [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')). The build needs Zig, Cargo,
and Deno.

To stay on your own machine with no iroh at all, use:

```sh
zig build rooms-web -Dtransport=tcp
```

That sends everything over plain TCP, with no encryption and no proof of who
is on the other end. Use it to look around, never to put Rooms on a network.

# Choose where your session lives

Open the page. The first time, Rooms asks where to keep your session: the
rooms you join, your direct conversations, your drafts, and your history
settings. Choose **Use this browser** to start.

[The first-run choice. Keep your session in this browser, or open one that another device or a server is already keeping.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-dark.webp'))

[The first-run choice. Keep your session in this browser, or open one that another device or a server is already keeping.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-light.webp'))

The choice is about who keeps your session, not what you can do with it. Keep
it here and it lives on this device. Share it, and whoever runs it, this page
or a server, keeps the one copy that every view opens. You can change your mind
later in Settings.

# Join a room

The join card already points at the server that gave you the page. Type a
room name such as `workshop`. If no room has that name, joining creates
it. You can leave **Display name** empty: the grey suggestion is a name the
server can give you right now, and joining with the field empty takes it.

[The join card, pointed at the server that gave you the page, with a room name typed. The display name can stay empty.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-join-dark.webp'))

[The join card, pointed at the server that gave you the page, with a room name typed. The display name can stay empty.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-join-light.webp'))

If the name you type is taken, the card shows the next free one and will not
send a join it knows would fail. Rooms never quietly changes a name you typed.

Now open the same address as somebody else (a private window is the quickest
way), choose **Use this browser** there too, and join `workshop` under another
name. Each session is its own peer, with its own seat in the room and its own
history. A bot makes a fine partner as well: the
[CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) joins a room from a terminal as
`glowcap apps rooms session`, which is how the bots on the
[Rooms]($link.page('rooms')) page got there.

# Talk in the room

Type into the box at the bottom and press Enter. Shift+Enter starts a new
line. You can attach files and use simple formatting, and the command menu
lists what you can do in this conversation.

A line you send shows as pending until the room confirms it. If the room
refused it before anything happened, Rooms puts your text back in the box.
If nobody can say whether it went through, Rooms tells you that instead of
inviting you to send it twice.

Hover one of your own lines to edit or delete it. You can, because the room
handed you the reference for that line when you posted it, not because your
name matches an account.

# Open a direct conversation

In the member list, press the message button beside the other member. The
server introduces you, and the new conversation appears under
**Direct messages**. From here on, your messages go straight between the two
of you, and the room sees none of them.

[A direct conversation, opened from the member list. The header says what this connection is, and the card above the first message says who can read it.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-direct-dark.webp'))

[A direct conversation, opened from the member list. The header says what this connection is, and the card above the first message says who can read it.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-direct-light.webp'))

By default the connection is WebRTC. It goes straight between you when it
can find a route, and uses STUN and TURN servers when the network needs
them. If it cannot connect where you are, open Settings, go to the **Reachability**
tab, and under **Direct message connection** pick **Through the room relay**
instead of **Browser to browser**. That setting only changes direct messages,
not how your session is shared.

[WebRTC signaling]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) explains offers,
candidates, STUN, and TURN. [Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations'))
shows where a host supplies TURN credentials and the relay.

# Open your session somewhere else

Back in the first session, open Settings and go to the **Session** tab.
**Share this session** publishes it and shows you its link. Open the page
somewhere else (a phone works well), choose **Shared session**
on the first screen, paste the link, and press **Attach**.

[The same session opened on a phone. The room, its members, and the direct conversation all came with it, and the divider marks where the shared history ends and live messages begin.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-attached-dark.webp'))

[The same session opened on a phone. The room, its members, and the direct conversation all came with it, and the divider marks where the shared history ends and live messages begin.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-attached-light.webp'))

The phone shows the same rooms, the same direct conversation, and the same
history settings, and a line posted from either side shows up in both.
Closing or reloading the page that shared the session ends the sharing.
For a session that is always on, run the session service from
[Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')) and open it the same way.

# Keep and share history

Rooms can keep room and direct-message history in your session. A host can
also keep room history on the server, run an archive service, or hand
someone a read-only link to an archive. These stay separate, and you choose
which one new messages go to.

Files stay with their messages. A history entry can describe an attachment
without claiming a file the archive never saved. The
[operations guide]($link.page('rooms/operations')) covers history on the
server, its limits, and repair.

# Continue

[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) explains what a click
promises and how the code is divided. To host Rooms or put it behind HTTPS,
continue with [Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')). To connect an
IRC network or client, use [IRC and Rooms]($link.page('rooms/irc')).
